  {
   "_id": 13181,
   "ANSWER": "<p data-end=\"2500\" data-start=\"2453\"><strong data-end=\"2466\" data-start=\"2455\">Answer (Explain in detail):</strong></p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li data-end=\"2600\" data-start=\"2467\">\n\t<p data-end=\"2600\" data-start=\"2469\"><strong data-end=\"2486\" data-start=\"2469\">Global Table:</strong> Uses ordered triples &lt;domain, object, rights-set&gt;; easy to implement but inefficient due to size and I/O needs.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"2733\" data-start=\"2601\">\n\t<p data-end=\"2733\" data-start=\"2603\"><strong data-end=\"2632\" data-start=\"2603\">Access Lists for Objects:</strong> Stores access lists per object, efficient in sparse matrices but requires default rights checking.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"2959\" data-start=\"2734\">\n\t<p data-end=\"2959\" data-start=\"2736\"><strong data-end=\"2769\" data-start=\"2736\">Capability Lists for Domains:</strong> Associates domains with object-capability pairs; direct access control but requires secure management to prevent unauthorized capability use.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"2959\" data-start=\"2734\">\n\t<p data-end=\"2959\" data-start=\"2736\"><strong>A Lock-Key Mechanism:</strong>&nbsp;lock-key scheme is a compromise between access lists and capability lists.</p>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n",
